Reversing a list flips every `next` arrow. Walk it with three pointers: `prev` (the reversed part so far), `cur` (the node being flipped), and `next` (the rest of the list).

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ListNode head = build(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
        show(head);
        head = reverse(head);
        show(head);
    }

    {{code}}

    static void show(ListNode head) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (ListNode n = head; n != null; n = n.next) {
            sb.append(n.val);
            if (n.next != null) {
                sb.append(" -> ");
            }
        }
        System.out.println(sb.length() == 0 ? "empty" : sb.toString());
    }

    static ListNode build(int... values) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode tail = dummy;
        for (int v : values) {
            tail.next = new ListNode(v);
            tail = tail.next;
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }
}

class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;
    ListNode(int val) {
        this.val = val;
    }
}
```

```java
static ListNode reverse(ListNode head) {
    ListNode prev = null;
    ListNode cur = head;
    while (cur != null) {
        ListNode next = cur.next;  // save the rest first
        cur.next = prev;           // flip the arrow
        prev = cur;                // grow the reversed part
        cur = next;                // move on
    }
    return prev;
}
```

Save `cur.next` before you overwrite it, or the rest of the list is lost. When `cur` falls off the end, `prev` sits on the old last node, which is the new head, so return it.

Always store the result: `head = reverse(head)`. Reversal changes the nodes themselves, so the old `head` variable now points at the new tail, whose `next` is `null`. It runs in one pass with no extra list, and works for an empty list too (it returns `null`).

Here the numbers go into an `int[]` first, and `build(values)` links them in order with a dummy head and a `tail` pointer. That way you can build two separate lists from one input.

