```java
ListNode slow = head;
ListNode fast = head;
while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
}
// slow is the middle
```

When `fast` reaches the end, `slow` has gone half as far, so it sits on the **middle**. For an even length like 1 2 3 4 it stops on 3, the second middle. Check `fast != null` before `fast.next`: the `&&` short-circuits, which avoids a `NullPointerException`.

**Floyd cycle detection** uses the same loop. If the tail links back into the list, `fast` never hits `null`. Once both are inside the cycle, `fast` closes the gap by one node per turn, so it can never jump over `slow` and they meet. Compare with `slow == fast`: here you want the very same node object, not equal values.

```java harness
